        <div class="getting-started-layout-main">
        <div class="full-width-section">
            <div class="section-text">
                <h1>Chapter 2 — Configuration</h1>
                <p><strong>Powerful control without complexity</strong></p>
                <p>One of the defining principles of Panels DCC is that <strong>everything is configured visually</strong>. There is no requirement to write scripts, edit configuration files, or understand complex command structures. If you can drag, drop, and click, you can build and operate a sophisticated DCC layout.</p>
                <p>From trains and accessories to full signalling-style panels, configuration happens directly in the interface, with immediate visual feedback.</p>

                <h2>Before you create an account or subscription</h2>
                <p>Cloud-based design and your account work together with <strong>PanelsDCC Connect</strong> and <strong>PanelsDCC Control</strong> on your Raspberry Pi. Complete <strong><a href="<%= basePath %>/getting-started/installation.html">Getting started</a></strong> (install software, Wi‑Fi, Connect, <a href="<%= basePath %>/getting-started/first-train.html">first train</a>, and <a href="<%= basePath %>/getting-started/account.html">your PanelsDCC account</a>) before you rely on a subscription.</p>
                <p>That means your command station is detected and controllable, and Control shows a healthy connection so you can drive at least one locomotive and one accessory as expected.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <hr/>

        <div class="content-section content-section-stacked" id="trains">
            <div class="section-text">
                <h2>Trains</h2>
                <p>Panels DCC draws on an extensive <strong>UK locomotive library</strong>, allowing new trains to be added simply by typing a description. Images and core details are loaded instantly, removing the need for manual data entry or hunting for reference material.</p>
                <p id="functions">For sound-fitted locomotives, Panels DCC includes a growing library of <strong>DCC function maps</strong>. Instead of remembering function numbers, you control features through <strong>clear, recognisable icons</strong> — lights look like lights, horns look like horns.</p>
                <p id="function-maps"><strong>Functions and Function Maps</strong> — create custom maps, upload icons, add functions to maps, and reorder so favourites appear first.</p>
                <p>Unlike many systems, Panels DCC also lets you:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Create your own custom functions and function maps</li>
                    <li>Upload and assign your own icons to functions and maps</li>
                    <li>Add functions to existing maps</li>
                    <li>Reorder functions so your favourites appear first</li>
                </ul>
                <p>This means the control interface adapts to <em>how you drive</em>, not the other way around.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="section-media section-media-fullwidth">
                <div class="section-media-placeholder">
                    Image/Video: Train config
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <hr/>

        <div class="content-section content-section-stacked" id="accessories">
            <div class="section-text">
                <h2>Accessories</h2>
                <p>Accessory configuration in Panels DCC is just as visual as train control.</p>
                <p>We provide a growing set of <strong>pre-built animated accessories</strong>, including:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Digital colour-light signals</li>
                    <li>Semaphore signals with realistic movement</li>
                    <li>Turnouts and other layout elements</li>
                </ul>
                <p id="macros">These aren't abstract symbols — they are designed to <em>look</em> like the real thing, making the state of the layout immediately obvious during operation.</p>
                <p><strong>Macros</strong> — group accessories and routes for repeatable operation where your layout supports it.</p>
                <p>You can also define your own accessories, using non-animated visuals that change automatically based on state.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="section-media section-media-fullwidth">
                <div class="section-media-placeholder">
                    Image/Video: Animated Accessories
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <hr/>

        <div class="content-section content-section-stacked" id="panels">
            <div class="section-text">
                <h2>Panels</h2>
                <p><strong>Panels</strong> are where your diagram meets your layout: you build them visually in the panel editor.</p>
                <p>Drag elements into place to match your trackwork and operating scheme. Then configure each element — for example, <strong>add signals to sections</strong> so the panel reflects how you run trains.</p>
                <p>Link those elements to accessories you have already defined, or <strong>create and configure a new accessory without leaving the panel editor</strong> when something is missing. The panel stays the centre of the work: layout, signals, and accessories stay connected in one place.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="section-media section-media-fullwidth">
                <div class="section-media-placeholder">
                    Image/Video: Panel Configuration Interface
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

